 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses 2Q eviction. A newly admitted sheet enters a first-access queue (A1); a second access before eviction promotes it to a reused queue (Am). Eviction draws from A1 first, then Am, each in least-recently-added order within its own queue. A1 is held to a fixed fraction of the 12 GiB budget, sized to absorb a long sequential screening without exhausting Am; the remainder of the budget is available to Am. The A1/Am size split is private replacement bookkeeping, tuned empirically rather than derived, and is not exposed to any other node.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (first-access queue A1, reused queue Am) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU's single recency ordering lets a long sequential screening evict sheets an editor still needs for comparison, because one-off scan traffic and reused sheets compete for the same position. Frequency-based aging protects repeat use but adds per-entry counter maintenance and can keep a clip resident after an editor has moved to a new project, since aging lags the switch. 2Q isolates one-off scan traffic in A1 so it cannot evict sheets with proven reuse in Am, matching the observed alternation between long screenings and repeated comparisons, at the cost of a second queue and an A1/Am size split that needs tuning. |
